<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest</AnalysisLevel>
    <Version>3.0.0-alpha.1</Version>
    <Company>SilexGIS</Company>
    <Product>SilexGIS</Product>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>AGPL-3.0-or-later</PackageLicenseExpression>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Five advisories against SixLabors.ImageSharp, accepted here by name rather than fixed.

    NPOI 2.7.6 brings ImageSharp 2.x in transitively. The advisories cover every ImageSharp release
    up to 4.1.1 and are first patched in 4.1.2, whose API NPOI 2.7.6 cannot use — so there is no
    version to move to. NPOI's own answer is 2.8.1, which drops ImageSharp for SkiaSharp, but 2.8.x
    requires accepting the Open Source Maintenance Fee EULA: a paid-licence commitment, which is a
    decision for the project's owner and not a way to fix a build.

    What is being accepted. All five are in image decoding and processing — ICC colour-profile
    parsing and histogram equalisation. Nothing in this solution decodes an image through NPOI: it
    is used by two classes, one writing spreadsheet cells and one extracting text from legacy
    Office files, and neither mentions a picture, an image or a drawing. The vulnerable code ships
    in the package and is not reached from here.

    Here rather than in the project that references NPOI, because an audit suppression does not
    cross a project reference: the API project sees the same transitive package and failed on it
    while Infrastructure was quiet.

    Named advisories and not the NU1902/NU1903 categories, deliberately — the next advisory against
    anything at all is still an error that stops the build, which is the only reason this one was
    noticed. A clean restore had begun failing while warm caches went on succeeding.

    Revisit when the owner has decided about the NPOI licence, or when a 2.7.x release asks for
    ImageSharp 4.1.2.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-gwg2-r3hj-4w44" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-j3p4-wp97-rph4" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-j9gm-c75j-xc9q" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-jjfr-hcj7-qf5w" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-wmxv-xphr-5c9g" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Twelve advisories against MessagePack 2.5.187, accepted here by name on the project owner's
    ruling.

    The package arrives with the survey library's Therion.Workspace module, which this solution
    references for the builder that wraps an uploaded Therion survey in a compilable project. The
    library declares MessagePack for one optional parse cache — a desktop editor's way of keeping
    parsed files on disk between sessions — and nothing this solution calls constructs it. The
    owner was asked whether the library should first be split, or the package bumped upstream, and
    ruled that the reference goes ahead with the advisories accepted instead.

    What is being accepted. Every one of the twelve is in reading MessagePack bytes: depth and
    length limits the deserialiser fails to enforce (nested arrays, Skip, union and JSON-conversion
    paths, multi-dimensional arrays, the Unity blit formatter), LZ4 decompression trusting a
    declared length, typeless and interface formatters bypassing their type and comparer
    restrictions, and the ASP.NET Core input formatter trusting request bodies. This solution reads
    no MessagePack bytes from anywhere, does not register that formatter, and no upload reaches the
    library's cache. All twelve are fixed within the same 2.5 line (2.5.301 for eleven, 2.5.303 for
    the nested-array one), so the version the library pins is the whole problem.

    Named advisories and not the NU1902/NU1903 categories, for the same reason as the block above:
    the next advisory against anything is still an error. Suppressed here, where every project of
    this solution reads it, because a suppression does not cross a project reference. The
    submodule's own projects import their repository's settings instead; they skip the audit, since
    every package they declare is audited again, with warnings as errors, in the projects that
    reference them (see external/Directory.Packages.props).

    Revisit, and delete this block, when the survey library moves MessagePack to 2.5.303 or later.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-2f33-pr97-265q" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-2x83-8g95-xh59" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-cj9g-3mj2-g8vv" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-cxmj-83gh-fp49" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-hv8m-jj95-wg3x" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-q2h6-ghwm-5qm8" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-qhmf-xw27-6rqr" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-qhrr-8q5h-9q3h" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-v72x-2h86-7f8m" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-vh6j-jc39-fggf" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-w567-gjr2-hm5j" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-wfr3-xj75-pfwh" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
